## Abstract

Martian Eco is a civilizational training system architecture designed to develop the human behavioral, cognitive, and ethical capabilities required for sustained multiplanetary existence. It is not a school. It is not a curriculum. It is a hybrid system at the intersection of educational theory, systems engineering, behavioral science, AI integration, and institutional governance.

This document constitutes the final blueprint, incorporating four iterative development cycles of structural critique, academic review, and research integration. Version 4.0 addresses all previously identified gaps: physical implementation architecture, epistemic authority hierarchy, selection equity protocols, psychological safety boundaries, simulation governance ethics, minimum viable prototype design, technical infrastructure specification, formal risk modeling, and institutional pathway strategy.

The central thesis is as follows: civilizational survival in a multiplanetary age depends not on information volume, but on behavioral adaptability, psychological resilience, ethical reasoning capacity, and cognitive stability under conditions of extreme uncertainty. Education systems designed for stable, single-planet contexts are structurally insufficient to meet this demand. Martian Eco is designed to replace that insufficiency with a rigorously engineered alternative.

