## Part X — Psychological Safety and Ethics Protocol

The introduction of simulation-based stress exposure, isolation protocols, and failure mode training into an educational framework requires explicit ethical boundaries, institutional safeguards, and harm prevention architecture. The absence of this architecture would render the psychological pillar professionally indefensible and institutionally unacceptable.

Martian Eco's Psychological Safety Protocol operates across four dimensions:

**Harm threshold definition.** All simulation exercises are designed to induce productive stress — challenge sufficient to develop genuine resilience — while remaining within defined physiological and psychological safety limits. Maximum simulation duration, intensity parameters, and psychological pressure thresholds are defined by the Independent Ethics Board in consultation with certified space and clinical psychologists. These parameters are reviewed annually and updated based on outcome data.

**Mandatory facilitation standards.** All psychological simulation exercises are conducted under the direct supervision of facilitators certified in space psychology protocols, crisis intervention, and ethical simulation facilitation. Facilitator-to-student ratios are defined by simulation intensity level.

**Opt-out and withdrawal rights.** Participation in simulation exercises above defined baseline intensity levels is voluntary. Students retain unconditional right to withdraw from any simulation exercise at any point without academic penalty, explanation requirement, or social stigma. Withdrawal procedures are simple, private, and non-judgmental by design.

**Welfare monitoring and post-simulation support.** Real-time welfare monitoring is standard for all exercises above baseline intensity. Structured post-simulation debrief is mandatory. Follow-up psychological support is available to all participants for a defined period following exercises. Longitudinal monitoring tracks cumulative psychological load across the student's time within the framework.

These protocols are not institutional risk management. They are expressions of the ethical commitment that the framework itself requires students to develop — a consistency between what is taught and how it is practiced.

