## Part XIII — Formal Risk Model

**Risk Category 1: Psychological Harm**

Risk: simulation exercises cause measurable adverse psychological outcomes in students. Mitigation: harm threshold architecture defined in Part X; mandatory facilitation standards; real-time welfare monitoring; opt-out rights; longitudinal load tracking. Residual risk: low with full protocol implementation. Escalation trigger: any serious adverse outcome triggers immediate protocol review by the Independent Ethics Board.

**Risk Category 2: Epistemic Failure**

Risk: AI systems generate and propagate false or biased knowledge that becomes embedded in curriculum. Mitigation: Reality Anchoring Layer; five-level epistemic hierarchy; human facilitator review of all AI-generated curriculum content; Scientific Advisory Commission oversight. Residual risk: moderate without continuous vigilance. Escalation trigger: any systematic misalignment between AI-generated curriculum content and Level 1-2 validation triggers a full curriculum audit.

**Risk Category 3: Governance Capture**

Risk: a single state, commercial entity, or ideological bloc acquires disproportionate influence over the framework's governance, selection, or curriculum. Mitigation: voting weight caps in Governing Council; funding diversification requirements; binding Independent Ethics Board jurisdiction; transparent public reporting; multi-bloc representation mandates. Residual risk: low with full governance architecture implementation. Escalation trigger: any single entity approaching governance weight thresholds triggers automatic structural review.

**Risk Category 4: Selection Bias Entrenchment**

Risk: Track B selection systematically disadvantages students from lower socioeconomic backgrounds or underrepresented regions, producing the technocratic elite the framework is explicitly designed to avoid. Mitigation: multi-dimensional selection criteria; socioeconomic neutralization protocols; geographic quotas; transparent reporting; annual equity audits by the Independent Ethics Board. Residual risk: moderate in the absence of sustained institutional attention. Escalation trigger: any annual equity audit finding systematic demographic imbalance in Track B selection triggers mandatory protocol revision.

**Risk Category 5: Simulation Governance Drift**

Risk: simulation environments gradually acquire quasi-prescriptive authority — their outcomes begin to influence real institutional decisions in ways that exceed their educational mandate. Mitigation: Principle 8 stated as a binding institutional constraint; explicit labeling of simulation outcomes as educational data only; prohibition on simulation results being used in any governance, policy, or selection decision outside the educational framework. Residual risk: low with rigorous governance enforcement.

