## Part IV — Physical Infrastructure

### The Martian Eco Learning Environment (MELE)

The primary physical infrastructure is designated the Martian Eco Learning Environment. It is not a conventional campus building. It is a purpose-configured hybrid space organized around four functional zones:

**Zone 1: The Science and Systems Lab**

A fully equipped wet and dry laboratory supporting hands-on experiment design, closed-loop systems prototyping, and materials analysis. Includes a dedicated ecological systems section where students design, build, and operate small-scale closed-loop food and water systems — functional analogs to the life support challenges of a Mars habitat. Physical experiment here provides the primary Level 1 epistemic anchor for AI-generated curriculum content.

**Zone 2: The Simulation Chamber**

A modular, reconfigurable enclosed environment capable of supporting isolation duration exercises, communication delay protocols, resource constraint scenarios, and habitat design challenges. The space is not theatrical. It is functional: designed for genuine psychological stress simulation at graduated intensity levels, with real-time facilitation monitoring stations, welfare observation capability, and rapid-exit protocols that are physically simple and psychologically non-stigmatizing.

The design draws directly on NASA's HERA analog — a 650-square-foot habitat designed to serve as a simulated spaceflight environment for research into isolation, confinement, and remote conditions — adapted for educational use with explicit safety boundaries, developmental staging, and ethical governance that research analog missions do not require in the same form. [The Mars Society](https://www.marssociety.org/news/2024/07/24/mdrs-director-sergii-iakymov-to-join-nasa-hera-simulation-in-houston/)

**Zone 3: The Governance and Ethics Forum**

A configurable collaborative space designed for governance simulation exercises, ethical reasoning workshops, cross-cultural negotiation scenarios, and long-horizon planning sessions. Equipped for multilingual participation, role-play documentation, and structured deliberation protocols. This is where interplanetary governance modules are brought to life — not through lecture, but through students actually attempting to negotiate resource allocation, draft legal frameworks, and resolve ethical disputes under simulated constraints.

**Zone 4: The Digital Integration Hub**

The primary interface between the physical MELE and the global digital network connecting satellite cohorts and partner institutions. Houses the AI curriculum platform terminals, assessment data visualization systems, collaborative project spaces with remote participants, and the epistemic audit workstations where students practice Reality Anchoring Layer verification methodology against live AI systems.

### Off-Site Analog Partnership

The MVP establishes a formal partnership with one existing analog mission facility for annual immersive expeditions available to Track B students. Facilities such as MDRS coordinate globally with analog habitats and research teams worldwide to test off-Earth living protocols — an established infrastructure that the MVP can access without replicating from scratch. [The Mars Society](https://www.marssociety.org/news/2025/10/17/mars-society-launches-2025-26-mdrs-field-season-with-crew-319-as-part-of-the-worlds-biggest-analog-project/) These expeditions are not tourist experiences. They are structured research rotations during which students carry genuine scientific workloads under simulated Mars mission protocols, generating data that feeds back into the MVP's assessment and curriculum systems.

