## Part V — Digital Platform Architecture

The MVP digital platform is a purpose-built system with five integrated components. It is not a modified version of an existing learning management system. The specific requirements of the Martian Eco framework — adaptive AI curriculum delivery with epistemic anchoring, longitudinal behavioral tracking, cross-cultural collaboration architecture, and simulation monitoring — cannot be met by any currently existing commercial platform without substantial custom development.

**Component 1: Adaptive Curriculum Engine**

A large language model-powered curriculum delivery system that generates personalized learning pathways across the six pillars, adapts to individual student progress and learning profile data, and creates varied problem scenario configurations to prevent assessment gaming. All AI-generated curriculum content is labeled by epistemic source level and flagged for human facilitator review before deployment in critical learning sequences. The engine does not self-update autonomously. All model updates pass through the Scientific Advisory Commission review cycle.

**Component 2: Assessment and Index Tracking System**

A longitudinal student profile system continuously capturing MCI, AEFS, and PSUIS data points across all learning activities, project outputs, simulation exercises, and peer review interactions. The system generates individual capability trajectories, cohort-level performance distributions, and equity monitoring dashboards for the Independent Ethics Board. Data governance follows the ownership and access protocols defined in the blueprint: students own their data, institutional access is aggregated and anonymized by default, external access requires Ethics Board approval.

**Component 3: Reality Anchoring Verification Interface**

A dedicated tool through which students perform epistemic audit exercises on AI-generated content. The interface presents AI-generated claims and prompts students to identify the appropriate validation method, locate Level 1 or Level 2 sources that confirm or challenge the claim, and document the verification process. This is not a background institutional safeguard. It is a regular, assessed learning activity — the primary mechanism through which AI epistemic fluency is developed practically rather than just conceptually.

**Component 4: Global Collaboration Network**

A secure, moderated platform connecting primary cohort students with satellite cohort participants across six countries. Designed with explicit counter-incentives against algorithmic filter bubbles: cross-cultural pairing in collaborative projects is assigned rather than self-selected, ensuring students work with peers whose cultural and educational contexts differ significantly from their own. The platform is not a social media environment. It is a structured collaborative workspace with defined project formats, peer review protocols, and facilitated reflection cycles.

**Component 5: Simulation Monitoring and Safety Console**

A real-time welfare monitoring system used by facilitators during all psychological simulation exercises above baseline intensity. Tracks behavioral indicators, time-in-simulation, self-reported wellbeing check-ins, and peer observation data. Generates automated alerts when predefined welfare thresholds are approached. The safety console is the technological implementation of the Psychological Safety Protocol — not a separate system, but the operational nervous system of the simulation architecture.

