## Part VI — Curriculum Design for the MVP

The MVP does not attempt to implement all six pillars at full depth simultaneously. A staged rollout across the three-year MVP cycle allows assessment instruments to be validated before being used for consequential decisions, and allows psychological simulation protocols to be refined before escalating to higher intensity levels.

### Year 1 — Foundation and Instrument Validation

Focus: Pillars I (Science), III (AI), and IV (Ecology). Psychological pillar introduced at baseline intensity only — no advanced simulation exercises in Year 1. Primary purpose: validate MCI and AEFS assessment instruments against a live cohort. Identify curriculum gaps and equity anomalies early. Establish the digital platform's stability under real use conditions. Build the student-facilitator trust relationships that are prerequisites for higher-intensity simulation work in Years 2 and 3.

Signature Year 1 Project — **The Closed-Loop Challenge:** Student teams design, build, and operate a small-scale closed-loop system capable of sustaining plant life for sixty days using only recycled water and waste-processed nutrients. This project integrates Pillars I and IV simultaneously, generates concrete physical experimental data for the Reality Anchoring Layer, and produces a clear, assessable output against which MCI subscores can be validated.

### Year 2 — Simulation Introduction and Governance Depth

Focus: All six pillars at graduated intensity. Psychological simulation protocols introduced at Level 1 and Level 2 — graduated isolation exercises of up to 48 hours, communication delay simulations, resource constraint scenarios. PSUIS instrument validation begins with Year 2 cohort data. Governance pillar introduced through structured simulation: students negotiate a simulated interplanetary resource dispute using actual international law frameworks as their starting material.

Signature Year 2 Project — **The Settlement Constitution:** Track B student teams, paired across satellite cohorts from different countries, draft a governance document for a hypothetical human settlement of 500 people on Mars. The document must address resource allocation, conflict resolution, environmental protection obligations, and the legal status of children born on Mars. Teams present and defend their constitutions before a panel of real governance and law faculty. The exercise is explicitly educational — its outputs carry no institutional authority — per Principle 8 of the blueprint.

### Year 3 — Full Integration and Longitudinal Assessment

Focus: Full six-pillar integration at maximum MVP depth. Advanced simulation exercises for Track B cohort under full Safety Protocol. First analog facility expedition for Track B students. Complete longitudinal assessment data collection enabling full instrument validation across the three-year development arc.

Signature Year 3 Project — **The 100-Year Plan:** The entire cohort — primary and satellite — collaborates on a civilizational planning exercise: designing a sustainable human civilization node capable of surviving for 100 years in isolation from Earth. The project requires simultaneous integration of all six pillars. It is the MVP's ultimate test of whether Martian Eco's core claim is valid: that students trained in this system can actually think and collaborate at civilizational scale.

Evidence from established project-based learning institutions shows that 95% of alumni report that project work prepared them for their careers and 88% report it helped them function effectively in the real world — validating PBL as the central pedagogy of the MVP curriculum design. [WORCESTER POLYTECHNIC INSTITUTE](https://www.wpi.edu/project-based-learning/project-based-education)

