## Part VIII — Success Metrics and Evaluation Framework

The MVP is evaluated against three tiers of metrics.

### Tier 1 — Instrument Validity Metrics

These metrics determine whether the assessment tools are working as designed. They are the highest-priority outputs of the MVP.

MCI validity: Cronbach's alpha above 0.75 across all MCI subscales; convergent validity established through correlation with established scientific reasoning assessments; discriminant validity confirmed through absence of significant correlation with unrelated constructs.

AEFS validity: Demonstrated predictive validity — AEFS scores at Year 1 must show statistically significant positive correlation with AI audit performance at Year 3. Construct validity established through expert panel review of item content against the five AI literacy dimensions.

PSUIS validity: Test-retest reliability established across paired simulation exercises with matched difficulty levels. Interrater reliability above 0.80 across facilitator behavioral observation scores. Convergent validity established through correlation with validated resilience instruments from space psychology literature.

### Tier 2 — Safety and Equity Metrics

These metrics determine whether the framework's ethical commitments are operationally real.

Psychological safety: Zero serious adverse outcomes attributable to simulation protocols. Student-reported psychological safety scores above 4.0 on a 5-point scale across simulation exercises. No statistically significant increase in validated distress indicators between pre- and post-simulation measurement points.

Equity: Track B selection demographics within 15% of the full cohort's demographic distribution across geographic, socioeconomic, and cultural dimensions. No statistically significant performance gap on MCI or AEFS attributable to socioeconomic background after controlling for educational resource access.

### Tier 3 — System Function Metrics

These metrics determine whether the system architecture is working.

Feedback loops: Curriculum revision cycles demonstrably informed by assessment data — documented evidence that Year 2 curriculum design incorporated findings from Year 1 assessment outputs.

AI epistemic anchoring: Student AEFS scores show increasing capacity to detect AI confabulation across the three-year arc. Facilitator audit of AI-generated curriculum content identifies and corrects epistemic level misattributions before student delivery in at least 95% of reviewed cases.

Global network function: Satellite cohort students complete cross-cultural collaborative projects with primary cohort students. Cross-cultural pairing produces measurable diversity of perspective in documented project outputs, as assessed by blind review panels.

